course: dictionary
lesson: 3295
title: Estimate: a careful guess
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To estimate is to make a careful guess about an amount, size, cost or time, without counting or measuring exactly. An estimate is the number or answer you get when you estimate. Estimate can be a verb or a noun.
explain: In maths, you estimate before you calculate, often by rounding. Round 38 to 40 and 21 to 20, and you expect about 60. If your answer is 600, you know it cannot be right. Estimating helps you check whether an answer is sensible.
explain: Look for clue phrases in the text: «estimate the answer», «a rough estimate», «a good estimate». A rough estimate is still a guess, not an exact count. Ask yourself: did the writer count every one, or did they make a careful guess?
fact: To estimate is to make a careful guess without counting or measuring exactly.
fact: An estimate is the number or answer you get by estimating.
fact: «Big numbers are always estimates.» Often nobody can count every single thing.
fact: «By some estimates China and India together made about half of the world's goods around 1700.» Here estimates are careful guesses, not exact totals.
fact: Estimating by rounding is a quick check: round the numbers, work it out, and see if your exact answer is close.
exercise: «Big numbers are always estimates.» What does estimate mean in this sentence? | Throwing the numbers away | *A careful guess, without counting every one | Counting every single one exactly | Writing the numbers in red
exercise: Ada needs 38 + 21. She rounds to 40 + 20 to estimate. What is her estimate? | 600 | 59 | 50 | *60
exercise: «By some estimates China and India together made about half of the world's goods around 1700.» What does estimates mean here? | *Careful guesses at amounts | Exact counts made by a machine | Places on a map | Old stories
test: Mia wants to know how long the bus ride takes, but she has no clock. What should she do? | *Estimate the time it will take | Count every second exactly | Sing a song | Ask the bus to stop
test: To check whether 49 × 21 is sensible, you estimate by rounding. What do you get? | 49 | *About 1000 | 70 | 2100
exam: Which sentence uses estimate as a noun? | We estimated the time | *Her estimate of the distance was about two kilometres | The pupils are estimating the weight | They must estimate how far it is
